---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { getPost } from "../../data/journal";

const post = getPost("screens-cut-blink-rate")!;
---

<Layout
  title={`${post.title} | EyesRhythm`}
  description={post.description}
  type="article"
  published={post.date}
  image={post.cover}
  imageAlt={post.coverAlt}
>
  <main id="main">
    <header class="cover">
      <img
        src={post.cover}
        alt={post.coverAlt}
        width="1280"
        height="720"
      />
      <div class="cover__shade"></div>
      <div class="cover__copy">
        <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker__dot"></span>{post.kicker}</p>
        <h1 class="display">{post.title}</h1>
        <p class="lede">{post.description}</p>
        <p class="meta">{post.read} · 4 September 2026</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="wrap article">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The work is going well, and then it isn’t, at least not for the
          eyes. The letters on the screen lose their edge.
          You rub once, keep typing, and by late afternoon the lids feel
          like paper.
        </p>
        <p>
          Brightness gets the blame. So does dry office air, or the familiar warning about “too much screen.” 
          But for many of us, screens are not optional. They are where the work happens.
          The less obvious problem is what happens while we are looking at them. We blink less, and the blinks we do make are often incomplete. 
          They come in bursts, separated by long pauses. During those pauses, the tear film covering the eye begins to thin.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nobody decides to blink less. Attention does it for us, and researchers have been measuring the effect for more than thirty years.
        </p>
        <p>
The response is not to stop using screens. It is to learn to notice what happens to your blinking and practice doing it more completely. With guidance, that can become a habit.
        </p>

        <h2>What a blink is actually doing</h2>
        <p>
          A blink is a wipe. The lids drag a thin film of tears across the
          eye, mix in oil from glands along the lid, and clear dust. Miss a
          few of those wipes and the film thins, then breaks. For a moment
          the surface of the eye is just sitting in the air.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            “The blinking mechanism plays a critical role in maintaining
            homeostasis of the ocular surface, tear film, and lacrimal
            functional unit.”
          </p>
          <cite>Wang, Power, Xue and Craig, 2025<sup><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup></cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          Blinking is how the front of the eye stays wet, fed, and
          protected. A late wipe, or a half wipe, leaves that system with
          less to work with. You look through the film. When it breaks, the
          letters on the screen are often the first thing that go soft.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/blink-tear.jpg"
            alt="Close-up of an open eye, a thin glossy tear film catching the light."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            After a full blink, a thin even film sits on the eye. That is
            the surface you are looking through.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>The rate is not a constant</h2>
        <p>
          People talk about “a normal blink rate” as if it were a resting
          heart rate. It isn’t. How often you blink depends on what the
          brain is doing with the eyes. Talk, sit quietly, read, stare at a
          monitor: each of those has its own pace.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            “Spontaneous eyeblink rate (SEBR) has been shown to be highly
            sensitive to cognitive and ambient conditions… cognitively
            demanding tasks such as reading and using visual display
            terminals have been documented to lower SEBR values.”
          </p>
          <cite>Cardona, Argilés and Pérez-Cabré, 2023<sup><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup></cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          Tsubota and Nakamori made this concrete in 104 office workers.
          They counted blinks while people were relaxed, while they read a
          book, and while they looked at a screen. Mean rates: 22 blinks a
          minute at rest, 10 with the book, 7 at the display.
          <sup><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>
          The book already halved the rate. The screen took more.
        </p>

        <figure class="rates" aria-label="Blink rate in three situations, Tsubota and Nakamori 1993">
          <div class="rate">
            <p class="rate__n">22</p>
            <p class="rate__unit">blinks / min</p>
            <p class="rate__label">Relaxed</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rate">
            <p class="rate__n">10</p>
            <p class="rate__unit">blinks / min</p>
            <p class="rate__label">Reading a book</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rate rate--low">
            <p class="rate__n">7</p>
            <p class="rate__unit">blinks / min</p>
            <p class="rate__label">Looking at a screen</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            Mean blink rates in 104 office workers. Tsubota and Nakamori,
            <cite>New England Journal of Medicine</cite>, 1993.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Two years earlier, in a smaller lab study, Patel and colleagues
          had already seen how far the drop can go:
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            “In normal healthy subjects, the results show an average 5-fold
            drop in blink rate during VDU use.”
          </p>
          <cite>Patel, Henderson, Bradley, Galloway and Hunter, 1991<sup><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup></cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          That is not a small dip. Eighteen blinks a minute in easy
          conversation can fall toward three or four in front of a display.
          The film then has seconds, not fractions of a second, to dry
          between wipes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Talking sits at the other extreme. Bentivoglio’s group recorded
          about 26 blinks a minute in conversation, 17 in quiet looking,
          and 4.5 while reading.<sup><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>
          Speech is a high-blink job. A page, or a screen, that has all of
          your attention is a low-blink job. Same pair of eyes. Different
          demand.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/blink-talk.jpg"
            alt="Two people talking across a table, looking at each other, not at screens."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Faces in conversation blink freely. Faces locked on a screen
            do not. The difference is the task, not the person.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Half a blink is not a blink</h2>
        <p>
          Counting how often the lids move still misses something. If they
          never quite meet, the wipe does not finish. The upper lid stops
          short, and a strip of the eye never gets the film renewed.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            “Previous studies have also highlighted that the diminished blink
            rate and increased tendency for incomplete blinking during tasks
            with high cognitive demand, such as digital screen use, can
            exacerbate dry eye symptoms and tear film instability.”
          </p>
          <cite>Wang, Power, Xue and Craig, 2025<sup><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup></cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          Wang’s group tested 453 adults and found that incomplete blinking
          tracked dry eye, as defined by a standard clinical checklist,
          more closely than blink rate did. For every extra 10% of blinks
          that failed to finish, the odds of that diagnosis rose by about
          12%. Incomplete blinking also lined up with a thinner oil layer
          and poorer quality of the lid glands.
          <sup><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>
        </p>
        <p>
          That is a mechanism, not a verdict on anyone reading this. Oil in
          the lids is squeezed out as the lids meet. A half blink squeezes
          less, spreads less, and leaves a dry patch sitting there.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/blink-incomplete.jpg"
            alt="Close-up of an eye whose upper lid has not fully met the lower lid."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            The lid comes down and stops. A strip of the eye never gets the
            wipe. That is an incomplete blink.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>The average can lie</h2>
        <p>
          Six blinks a minute sounds like a single number. It isn’t. Six
          blinks spaced through the minute, and six blinks piled into two
          clumps with a long wait in the middle, produce the same average
          and two very different hours for the eye.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cardona, Argilés and Pérez-Cabré call the evenness of those gaps
          <strong>blink regularity</strong>. They measure it as the
          coefficient of variation of the interblink interval: how much the
          waits between blinks wander, relative to their mean.
          <sup><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>
        </p>
        <p>
          To show why that matters, they drew four one-minute patterns that
          all had the same average gap of 12 seconds, and the same six
          blinks. Even, complete blinks left the surface exposed for about
          17% of the minute. The same six blinks, arriving in clumps with a
          long empty stretch, pushed exposure above 50%. Incomplete blinks
          on top of the clumps pushed it higher still.
          <sup><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>
          The count had not changed. The uncovered time had.
        </p>

        <figure class="tracks" aria-label="Even blinks versus clustered blinks over one minute">
          <p class="tracks__title">Same six blinks. Different minute.</p>
          <div class="track">
            <p class="track__label">Even</p>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 600 36" role="img" aria-hidden="true">
              <line x1="8" y1="18" x2="592" y2="18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"></line>
              <circle cx="8" cy="18" r="6" fill="#0071e3"></circle>
              <circle cx="124" cy="18" r="6" fill="#0071e3"></circle>
              <circle cx="240" cy="18" r="6" fill="#0071e3"></circle>
              <circle cx="356" cy="18" r="6" fill="#0071e3"></circle>
              <circle cx="472" cy="18" r="6" fill="#0071e3"></circle>
              <circle cx="592" cy="18" r="6" fill="#0071e3"></circle>
            </svg>
            <p class="track__note">A blink about every 12 seconds</p>
          </div>
          <div class="track">
            <p class="track__label">Lumpy</p>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 600 36" role="img" aria-hidden="true">
              <line x1="8" y1="18" x2="592" y2="18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"></line>
              <circle cx="8" cy="18" r="6" fill="#e07a72"></circle>
              <circle cx="68" cy="18" r="6" fill="#e07a72"></circle>
              <circle cx="98" cy="18" r="6" fill="#e07a72"></circle>
              <circle cx="512" cy="18" r="6" fill="#e07a72"></circle>
              <circle cx="542" cy="18" r="6" fill="#e07a72"></circle>
              <circle cx="592" cy="18" r="6" fill="#e07a72"></circle>
            </svg>
            <p class="track__note">A cluster, a long wait, another cluster</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            After Cardona, Argilés and Pérez-Cabré, 2023. The average looks
            the same. The long gap is where the surface dries.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Johnston and colleagues looked at those same gaps, the interblink
          interval, in people with and without dry eye while they watched a
          documentary. Mean interval: 5.97 seconds in the normal group,
          2.56 seconds in the dry-eye group. They also found “a greater
          variability of interblink intervals in normal subjects.”
          <sup><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>
        </p>
        <p>
          Comfortable blinking is not a metronome, and it is not a panic.
          It can wait, then catch up.
          Irritated eyes often do the opposite: more blinks, tighter
          spacing, as if the surface is asking for another wipe. A long
          screen day can shove a comfortable eye the other way, toward
          fewer blinks and longer waits.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            “The tendency towards decreased blink rate during visual function
            tasks, such as computer use, reading, video gaming, and watching
            TV can exacerbate the signs and symptoms of dry eye.”
          </p>
          <cite>Johnston, Rodriguez, Lane, Ousler and Abelson, 2013<sup><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <h2>Why the brain does this</h2>
        <p>
          A blink is a blackout, even if it lasts only a fraction of a
          second. When the work on the screen is dense, the brain either
          parks those blackouts between important moments or holds them
          back altogether. You keep seeing. The film waits.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nakano and colleagues found that people watching the same video
          tended to blink in sync, at the moments when they were least
          likely to miss what mattered.<sup><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>
          Cardona’s group described “an internal mechanism to regulate the
          most appropriate time for a blink in order to minimize the chance
          of losing critical information.”
          <sup><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>
        </p>
        <p>
          That helps the work. It does not help the film. You did not
          forget to blink. You were busy, and the lids waited.
        </p>

        <h2>What this means at a desk</h2>
        <p>
          Three things, working together, explain a lot of that late-day
          grit.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>The rate falls.</strong> Hard reading cuts blinks.
            Screens often cut them further.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The blinks go shallow.</strong> Incomplete ones leave a
            strip unwiped and spread less of the oil layer.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The timing gets lumpy.</strong> The same number of
            blinks, packed into clusters, can leave a long dry wait in the
            middle of the minute.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            “A complete characterization of blink parameters is relevant to
            better understand ocular dryness related to surface exposure and
            to advice patients towards a reeducation of their blinking
            habits.”
          </p>
          <cite>Cardona, Argilés and Pérez-Cabré, 2023<sup><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup></cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          Count is not enough. How fully the lids meet, and how even the
          gaps are, belong in the same picture. Cardona’s group also noted
          “the large percentage of users reporting ocular dryness during
          and after prolonged computer work.”
          <sup><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>
          Anyone who has finished a long day at a laptop has already felt
          that sentence.
        </p>
        <p>
          A screen is not poison, and none of this is a diagnosis. It is a
          description of a particular hour: still face, high attention,
          lids on hold. A look away, a full close, a slightly more even
          rhythm are small physical things, the way a stretch is a small
          thing for a stiff neck.
        </p>
        <p>
          EyesRhythm is a
          <a href="/blink-rate-detector">blink rate detector</a>
          built around that picture. It watches how often
          you blink and how even the gaps are, on this computer. Video
          stays here. It is a desk companion, not a clinic. The
          <a href="/journal/count-is-not-rhythm">score in the window</a>
          is how those two things become one glance. If the eyes stay
          sore, or vision changes, that is a conversation for an
          eye-care professional.
        </p>
        <p>
          The camera has to see the eyes first. Sit as you usually work;
          the <a href="/journal/camera-setup">setup guide</a> is short.
        </p>

        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ol class="sources" id="sources">
          <li id="ref-1">
            Wang MTM, Power B, Xue AL, Craig JP. Blink completeness and rate
            in dry eye disease: an investigator-masked, prospective
            registry-based, cross-sectional, prognostic study.
            <cite>Contact Lens and Anterior Eye</cite>. 2025;48:102369.
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.clae.2025.102369">doi:10.1016/j.clae.2025.102369</a>
          </li>
          <li id="ref-2">
            Cardona G, Argilés M, Pérez-Cabré E. Loss of blink regularity
            and its impact on ocular surface exposure.
            <cite>Diagnostics</cite>. 2023;13:2362.
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics13142362">doi:10.3390/diagnostics13142362</a>
          </li>
          <li id="ref-3">
            Tsubota K, Nakamori K. Dry eyes and video display terminals.
            <cite>New England Journal of Medicine</cite>. 1993;328:584.
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM199302253280817">doi:10.1056/NEJM199302253280817</a>
          </li>
          <li id="ref-4">
            Patel S, Henderson R, Bradley L, Galloway B, Hunter L. Effect of
            visual display unit use on blink rate and tear stability.
            <cite>Optometry and Vision Science</cite>. 1991;68(11):888–892.
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1097/00006324-199111000-00010">doi:10.1097/00006324-199111000-00010</a>
          </li>
          <li id="ref-5">
            Bentivoglio AR, Bressman SB, Cassetta E, Carretta D, Tonali P,
            Albanese A. Analysis of blink rate patterns in normal subjects.
            <cite>Movement Disorders</cite>. 1997;12(6):1028–1034.
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/mds.870120629">doi:10.1002/mds.870120629</a>
          </li>
          <li id="ref-6">
            Johnston PR, Rodriguez J, Lane KJ, Ousler G, Abelson MB. The
            interblink interval in normal and dry eye subjects.
            <cite>Clinical Ophthalmology</cite>. 2013;7:253–259.
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.2147/OPTH.S39104">doi:10.2147/OPTH.S39104</a>
          </li>
          <li id="ref-7">
            Nakano T, Yamamoto Y, Kitajo K, Takahashi T, Kitazawa S.
            Synchronization of spontaneous eyeblinks while viewing video
            stories. <cite>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</cite>.
            2009;276:3635–3644.
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2009.0828">doi:10.1098/rspb.2009.0828</a>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="note">
          EyesRhythm is a wellness companion, not a medical device. It is
          not intended to diagnose, treat, or prevent disease. Persistent
          discomfort or a change in vision belongs with a licensed eye-care
          professional.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="back">
        <a href="/journal">All journal</a>
        ·
        <a href="/blink-rate-detector">Blink rate detector</a>
        ·
        <a href="/journal/count-is-not-rhythm">Why the blink count is not enough</a>
        ·
        <a href="/journal/camera-setup">Camera setup</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .cover {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: min(72vh, 640px);
    display: grid;
    align-items: end;
    color: #fff;
    background: #1d1d1f;
  }

  .cover img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .cover__shade {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15) 20%,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.62) 100%
    );
  }

  .cover__copy {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    width: min(var(--wrap), calc(100% - var(--gutter) * 2));
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 48px 0 40px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .cover .kicker {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86);
  }

  .cover .display {
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.2rem);
    max-width: 16ch;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .cover .lede {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.88);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }

  .meta {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7);
  }

  .article {
    padding: 48px 0 96px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .shot {
    margin: 16px 0 4px;
    width: min(100%, 42rem);
  }

  .shot img {
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: 20px;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .shot figcaption,
  .rates figcaption,
  .tracks figcaption {
    margin-top: 8px;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  blockquote {
    margin: 20px 0;
    padding: 4px 0 4px 18px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--blue);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  blockquote p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  blockquote cite {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 8px;
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  .prose sup a {
    color: var(--blue-deep);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .rates {
    margin: 20px 0 8px;
    width: min(100%, 42rem);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 10px;
  }

  .rate {
    background: var(--band);
    border-radius: 20px;
    padding: 20px 12px 16px;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .rate--low {
    background: #eef4fb;
  }

  .rate__n {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 2.4rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.05em;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .rate__unit,
  .rate__label {
    margin: 6px 0 0;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  .rates figcaption {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
  }

  .tracks {
    margin: 20px 0 8px;
    width: min(100%, 42rem);
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
    padding: 20px 20px 16px;
    background: var(--band);
    border-radius: 20px;
  }

  .tracks__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .track {
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  .track__label {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  .track svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: 36px;
    color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.12);
  }

  .track__note {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  .sources {
    margin: 8px 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    max-width: 42rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  .sources a {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .note {
    margin-top: 24px;
    max-width: 42rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  .back {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .back button {
    color: var(--blue-deep);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .rates {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
